    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As news of yet another suicide attempt of yet another college student at the University of Mauritius (UoM) breaks locally, the uproar on social media and the trend of #MentalHealth picks up and goes down after a few days. Researchers have noted that there is a huge stigma attached to mental well-being, and even though counselors are provided on campus, those resources are not being used. Despite some research done on academic pressures, there is a lack of literature on the intersectionality of identities and roles, cultural distress, the transitional stage of adulthood, institutionalized stressors, and the need to belong at university. The purpose of this study is to understand the different stressors embedded within Mauritian institutions through the lenses of adulthood for UoM students, and how the different social identities and factors play into shaping their overall well-being. Through in-depth ethnographic interviews and surveys of thirteen college students, I try to identify pressures present in their daily lives and how college students express stress, which I examine using anthropological theories. I explore topics including politics, religion, infrastructure, and family, which came up during my interviews. This research gives an insight into the stressors that put pressures on the UoM student and attempts to address the gap in literature when it comes to stress among young adults in Mauritius. Hopefully, this study contributes to implementing better strategies to best deal with the wellbeing of young emerging adults in Mauritius.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Rughoobur, I. (2023). The Trap Called “Youth”: A Mauritian Ethnography on Adulthood.. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/vj287vt4296. https://doi.org/10.25740/vj287vt4296.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
